                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "026b638e-39c8-3783-a584-5aab4a46445f",
                        "entry_id": "55451de5-c7ec-3c51-806d-9d38ddb0ae3a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The single aster formation that forms at the equatorial plate at the end of metaphase in mitosis."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fd733d3e-e90e-4a40-a7ee-45b9f7eff2e8",
                                "sense_id": "026b638e-39c8-3783-a584-5aab4a46445f",
                                "text": "After the encounter of the two pronuclei, one quadrant of the former positive cross of the monaster is missing on the side of the egg pronucleus."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bfabbf83-a8ab-4ae3-b4ae-32450c21e494",
                                "sense_id": "026b638e-39c8-3783-a584-5aab4a46445f",
                                "text": "One finds the longest chromosomes in the vascular epithelial cells if the monaster is well developed (Fig. 24), whilst shortly beforehand (Fig. 15) the chromosomes are still rather short."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ab85600e-3f48-4c4a-b6c8-e1eea83916ee",
                                "sense_id": "026b638e-39c8-3783-a584-5aab4a46445f",
                                "text": "At the same time the middle piece breaks down and releases the centriole, around which egg cytoplasm forms the sperm monaster."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f36733c7-fe71-4bee-9ddc-5415ead8dcf5",
                                "sense_id": "026b638e-39c8-3783-a584-5aab4a46445f",
                                "text": "Large monasters can be produced in egg cells by a variety of methods; drugs, such as strychnine or chloral hydrate (Hertwig, 1887 ), hypertonic sea water (Morgan, 1896; Wilson,1901;Herlant, 1918),and by mechanical agitation (Boveri, 1903; Painter, 1915)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
